{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1178400-what-about-those-animal-stats","id":1178400,"title":"What about those animal stats","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-01-23T04:15:38-08:00","id":1178400,"title":"What about those animal stats","content":"Hello all. I'm sure this has come up before, but I have to ask anyway.<br /> <br /> What's up with the animal stats?<br /> <br /> I looked over the sheet linked from the sticky post in this forum and they seem to have the same issues as was there in nWoD 1.0. This seem especially relevant for Vampire (which is why I ask here), as there are several powers that deal with animals. One of my players particularly aims to gather quite a few number of familiars around her.<br /> <br /> So, is there anyone who has looked over the animal stats into something more realistic? Or has a way of interpreting them that I'm not aware of.<br /> <br /> I mean, based on the stats as listed, a Raven is as intelligent as an average human. That just can't be right. And a large dog or wolf still has Intelligence 1? So they're as clever as a dumb human? Humans can be pretty dumb but they're still far more intelligent than any dog I've ever met. Not to mention that a Raven raised as a familiar with Animalism 2 would get a reduction in Intelligence. What's up with that?<br /> <br /> And then we haven't touched the combat stats which often seem quite far out there as well. Wolves, for example, seem to be far more dangerous opponents than a well-trained human. I don't doubt that they can dangerous, but humans have fought off wolves for millennia. Which doesn't seem very plausible given their extremely high Strength and Defense values. Is there any reason why a large dog or wolf has Athletics 4? Is there no difference between a basic dog and one that's been well trained? Do they really have to be (and is it realistic that they are) as good as a well-trained athlete? Not to mention that most likely the only thing they can do is run and jump which only describes like 1/10th of the Athletics skill.<br /> <br /> From my perspective, it seems as the weird animal stats are mostly caused by trying to use mechanics made for human (and supernatural) creates. It simply doesn't scale down very well. Wouldn't it be better to either have animals on a completely separate type of mechanic, or no mechanics at all? Just give them dice pools for specific things such as &quot;Attack with bite, 6 dice&quot;, &quot;Defense: 4&quot; and &quot;Resist mental effect: 2&quot;. Can human stats really be used to portray animals?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5082-lorsa","name":"Lorsa","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-23T04:30:57-08:00","id":1178402,"title":"","content":"The Chronicles of Darkness rules do expect you to apply common sense. I mean, yes, the mechanical backbone is tuned to representing humans and becomes a bit of a kludge applied to wild animals. But Attribute and Skill ratings are just numbers of dice you roll, and the book knows that you know, no matter what number they put next to Intelligence in a stat block, that a raven isn't going to be flying into a library and rolling to research things or perform forensic investigation. The context in which these traits are applicable is different from humans in ways that are more impactful than whether they roll 1 or 3 dice at target number eight. The benefit for an animal familiar is expanding the context in which it can perform actions.<br /> <br /> (I do think there are imperfections here; I probably wouldn't have Animalism specify an Intelligence rating for the animal. Just the context that it can understand complex language is enough.)<br /> <br /> The dice pool approach <i>is</i> generally a better approach for the kind of NPC roles that animals normally occupy. But that does get muddied some by the fact that these stat blocks are meant to serve double duty as both animal encounters and stats for various shapeshifting powers, like Protean 3.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}